 ## Overview
-Outer/Inner Shell Classification is a voxelization preprocessing step that partitions polygonal geometry into boundary-aligned outer shells and internal inner shells to optimize occlusion culling and rendering. It separates polygons into six groups based on coplanarity with the voxel grid boundaries (X=0, X=1000, Y=0, Y=1000, Z=0, Z=1000) and one group for all other internal geometry. Unlike methods that project all geometry for boundary occlusion, this method restricts 2D boundary masks to only those polygons coplanar with the boundaries, preventing interior polygons from incorrectly occluding the shell [S1].
+Outer/Inner Shell Classification is a voxelization preprocessing step that partitions polygonal geometry into boundary-aligned outer shells and internal inner shells to optimize occlusion culling and rendering [S1]. It separates polygons into six groups based on coplanarity with the voxel grid boundaries ($X=0$, $X=1000$, $Y=0$, $Y=1000$, $Z=0$, $Z=1000$) and one group for all other internal geometry [S1]. Unlike methods that project all geometry for boundary occlusion, this method restricts 2D boundary masks to only those polygons coplanar with the boundaries, preventing interior polygons from incorrectly occluding the shell [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs:** A polygonal mesh (Shape) defined in a local coordinate space with boundaries at $X \in [0, 1000]$, $Y \in [0, 1000]$, $Z \in [0, 1000]$.
-**Outputs:** Six outerShell coverage masks (2D bitmasks of size $1000 \times 1000$) and a list of retained innerShell 3D polygons.
